{"answer_key_points":["A partial index is an index built over a subset of a table, where the subset is defined by a conditional expression called the predicate of the index; the index contains entries only for rows satisfying the predicate.","One major reason for using a partial index is to avoid indexing common values: a query searching for a common value will not use the index anyway, so keeping those rows out reduces the size of the index, speeds up queries that do use the index, and speeds up many table update operations."],"answerable":true,"category":"single_fact","chunking_version":"s2-chunk-v1","expected_reason_code":null,"paraphrase_group_id":"development-human-001","question":"In PostgreSQL 17.11, what is a partial index, and what is one major reason the documentation gives for using one?","question_id":"s4-dev-008","relevant_source_refs":[{"anchor":"INDEXES-PARTIAL","normalized_url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/17/indexes-partial.html","version":"17.11"}],"requested_version":"17.11","resolved_chunk_ids":["chk_0af3fb48da984b21ddcbc08a15e5e1ba166e51dc235393b3428b7a6eed551829","chk_36e9cdbf6d62464622f578787b4351216c99da72128f3b4299fb62fbb5dc8cbf","chk_5d00883adaf49889a040c4979443ef48fc6309f97e05936770921372615ebe77","chk_831e7bca44c7266839756988a85d261bd296818b7321dd519889e93b6fa05aab","chk_a014440b649d491228017bc0cc6e641c31d404de5d14b204c954bb7b93125e25","chk_bcd7d78497e9ae5b0c57369c1231324c8549c38ea8ba43c389ae9f5021dc21f9","chk_fadb868e4d21ca9d32ef0b0966ae0ad30415c70e142e130d5aceb60125c71a09"],"review_status":"human_reviewed","schema_version":1,"source":"human_written","split":"development"}
