{"answer_key_points":["pg_dumpall is a utility for writing out (“dumping”) all PostgreSQL databases of a cluster into one script file. The script file contains SQL commands that can be used as input to psql to restore the databases. It does this by calling pg_dump for each database in the cluster. pg_dumpall also dumps global objects that are common to all databases, namely database roles, tablespaces, and privilege grants for configuration parameters. (pg_dump does not save these objects.)"],"answerable":true,"category":"how_to_parameter","chunking_version":"s2-chunk-v1","expected_reason_code":null,"paraphrase_group_id":"development-how_to_parameter-014","question":"In PostgreSQL 17.11, what is PG-DUMPALL used for in the “Description” section?","question_id":"s4-dev-014","relevant_source_refs":[{"anchor":"APP-PG-DUMPALL-DESCRIPTION","normalized_url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/17/app-pg-dumpall.html","version":"17.11"}],"requested_version":"17.11","resolved_chunk_ids":["chk_45b79a57981edd5d28ebfe2b12ba5c69cf92331bb7e43d1828f9dd8b2a6be0f0"],"review_status":"human_reviewed","schema_version":1,"source":"assistant_curated","split":"development"}
